Scope of this work
------------------

cronoscope is **not a fork** of any single upstream project. It is an
independent tool that composes three libraries at runtime:

  * cron-parser  5.10.2  MIT  Copyright (c) 2014-2023 Harri Siirak
        used as the execution oracle: it decides which instants a cron
        expression fires. No source is copied into this repository.
  * cRonstrue    3.38.0  MIT  Copyright (c) 2017 Brady Holt
        used as the description engine: it turns an expression into natural
        language in English and Chinese. No source is copied.
  * luxon        3.7.2   MIT  Copyright (c) 2019 JS Foundation and other
        contributors
        used for the IANA time zone database and wall-clock arithmetic.
        No source is copied.

All three are consumed as pinned npm dependencies and bundled unmodified into
`demo/bundle.js` for the browser build. Their licence texts are reproduced
verbatim under `third_party/<package>/LICENSE`, hashed in
`third_party/INDEX.json`, and reprinted in full in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`.
No upstream file has been modified, so no upstream copyright notice needs to be
altered; the notices are simply carried along.

What is original to this project
--------------------------------

Everything under `src/` and `test/`, plus `demo/`, `scripts/` and `docs/`:

  * the nominal field model (`src/expression.js`) that reconstructs the
    wall-clock readings an expression asks for, including the left-padding that
    cron-parser applies to short expressions;
  * the wall-clock resolver (`src/wallclock.js`) that classifies a reading as
    unique / ambiguous / nonexistent and enumerates the instants;
  * the audit engine (`src/audit.js`) that diffs the nominal model against the
    engine's output slot by slot;
  * collision analysis, the four export targets, the CLI and the browser UI.

